//
// The tsconfig `next build` uses, and ONLY `next build` — wired via
// `typescript.tsconfigPath` in next.config.ts. `tsc --noEmit` (the `check-types` task)
// still reads tsconfig.json and still type-checks everything, tests included.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. The production image installs a PRUNED dependency closure
// (apps/console/Dockerfile: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --filter console... --filter
// @alethia/ee`), so no sibling app's node_modules exist inside it. Meanwhile tsconfig.json's
// `include` is `**/*.ts`, which picks up `tests/`, and tsc follows imports BEYOND `include`.
// `tests/lib/marketing-proxy.test.ts` imports `../../../marketing/proxy`, which imports
// `next/server` — resolvable in a full workspace install, absent in the pruned one:
//
//     ../marketing/proxy.ts(4,48): error TS2307: Cannot find module 'next/server'
//     Failed to type check.
//
// That failed EVERY production console build while CI stayed green, because CI's
// `turbo run check-types` runs after an unfiltered `pnpm install` where marketing's
// node_modules are present. The image and CI were type-checking the same files against
// different dependency graphs.
//
// The fix is not to make the import resolve. It is that a production image has no business
// type-checking test files at all: they are not shipped, they are already checked in CI, and
// compiling them is what dragged a sibling app into a build that deliberately excludes it.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  // `exclude` REPLACES the base's rather than merging, so node_modules is repeated here
  // deliberately — dropping it would widen the build's graph, not narrow it.
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "tests"]
}
